181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Predicting the next word in a sequence of text is a foundational task in natural language processing, with widespread applications in predictive keyboards, auto-completion systems, and conversational writing assistants. Smart Predictor is a deep learning-based text generation system that predicts the most probable succeeding word given a sequence of preceding words in an English text corpus. The system utilizes a sequential recurrent neural network developed using TensorFlow and Keras, consisting of an Embedding layer for dense word representations, an LSTM \(Long Short-Term Memory\) layer with 128 units to capture sequential context across word tokens, a Dropout layer for regularization, and a Dense softmax output layer spanning the vocabulary space. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (A structured training corpus derived from English literary and conversational texts is preprocessed by tokenizing words into integer indices, generating n-gram sliding window sequences, and padding sequences to a fixed maximum length. The model is trained using the Adam optimizer and categorical cross-entropy loss over multiple epochs. An interactive prediction function accepts an input phrase, tokenizes and pads the input, queries the trained model to output probability distributions over the vocabulary, and predicts the top-k next words with associated confidence scores. A comprehensive evaluation script computes test perplexity, top-1 and top-5 prediction accuracies, and categorical cross-entropy loss. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 123 0 R
/E (Experimental results demonstrate that the LSTM network successfully learns syntactic and semantic dependencies from the training corpus, achieving stable convergence and robust next-word predictions. The findings highlight the effectiveness of recurrent neural networks in modeling sequential text patterns while outlining limitations related to vocabulary size and out-of-vocabulary handling. Future enhancements include scaling the corpus to larger text datasets, incorporating pre- trained word embeddings \(such as GloVe or Word2Vec\), and exploring advanced Transformer-based architectures. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
