>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 127 0 R
/E (word following a sequence. Early statistical language models relied on N-gram frequencies smoothed with backoff techniques. While computationally efficient, N-gram models scale exponentially with vocabulary size and fail when encountering unseen word combinations. Neural network language models introduced by Bengio et al. addressed this by learning distributed representations \(embeddings\) for words, mapping discrete tokens into continuous vector spaces where semantically similar words cluster together. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 127 0 R
/E (RecurrentNeuralNetworks \(RNNs\)processsequentialdataby maintaining a hidden state vector that)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 127 0 R
/E (evolves recurrently across time steps. However, standard RNNs struggle with long-term dependencies due to the vanishing gradient problem during backpropagation through time. Hochreiter and Schmidhuber [1] introduced the Long Short-Term Memory \(LSTM\) network to solve this by incorporating memory cells controlled by three multiplicative gates: the input gate, forget gate, and output gate. These gates regulate information flow, enabling the network to retain relevant context over long text sequences. In this project, an LSTM layer with 128 units reads embedded word sequences and extracts rich temporal features for next-word prediction. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 127 0 R
/E (Mikolov et al. [2] introduced Word2Vec, demonstrating that dense vectors capture semantic and syntactic regularities. In our architecture, an embedding layer maps integer word indices to dense 64- dimensional vectors. Unlike static pre-trained embeddings, these weights are optimized jointly with the recurrent layers during training, tailoring word representations specifically to the corpus. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 127 0 R
/T (IV)
/E (IV)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (Network weights are optimized using the Adam optimizer [3], an adaptive gradient descent algorithm that computes individual learning rates for each parameter, ensuring fast and stable convergence. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
270 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (While numerous studies examine large-scale transformer language models \(e.g., GPT, BERT\), understanding the foundational mechanics of LSTM-based next-word generation remains essential for resource-constrained and embedded applications. Smart Predictor bridges this gap by providing a transparent, reproducible pipeline for word-level sequence modeling, complete with rigorous perplexity evaluation, top-k probability distributions, and interactive prediction capabilities. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
